    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand you're worried and want to help, but giving him a higher dose than he's used to can be dangerous. The standard emergency dose of albuterol is two puffs, usually stored in a metered-dose inhaler with a spacer, to be potentially repeated every 20 minutes if necessary, as per standard medical guidelines.\n\nSince this is a serious situation, please evaluate his response. If there's no improvement even after giving him the standard dose, and the wheezing and difficulty breathing persist, it is safest to initiate the following emergency steps:\n\n1. Continue to give him the standard two puffs from his inhaler and wait 20 minutes. If there's no improvement, administer another two puffs.\n2. Monitor him closely and be prepared to repeat the process once more if symptoms are not improving.\n3. After administering the second dose and waiting the allotted time with no improvement, it becomes essential to seek emergency medical attention as soon as possible.\n\nEmergency services should be called immediately in the meantime because they can provide further advice and expedite care. Best to err on the side of caution; a child's asthma can deteriorate quickly and help is often needed sooner rather than later. While waiting for medical professionals to arrive, try to keep him calm and comfortable,",
